  "text": "Subscribe to skip adsAdvertisementEvolutionAncient DNA May Reveal the Surprising Roots of English and Other Germanic LanguagesIron Age people were on the moveJCBy Jake Currie5:00 PM CST on November 5, 2026Add Nautilus to GoogleThe origins of the Germanic languages (English, German, Dutch, and the Nordic languages) have been the subject of debate for decades among linguists. Now geneticists are getting in on the action with a surprising new study published in Nature. Powered By 10 ShareNextStayAn international team of researchers led by geneticists at the University of Copenhagen analyzed more than 4,500 ancient genomes (712 of which had never been sequenced before). By matching up identical portions of the genetic sequences, they uncovered a previously unknown migration pattern from a mysterious group into Scandinavia around 4,000 years ago. The timing matches up with the spread of Germanic languages, as well as cultural practices like burial rites, architecture, runic carvings, and pottery crafting.Read more: “Was Human Language Born Around the Campfire?”Already have an account?Log inRead without limitsFinish this story and enjoy unlimited, ad-free access to Nautilus. Plans start at $6.17/month, billed annually.View plans Subscribe to skip adsAdvertisement",
